<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of North Carolina</h1><p class="st-meta">1712 CE – 1776 CE · capital New Bern</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British colony between Virginia and South Carolina, a land of small farms and naval stores, where the Regulator revolt foreshadowed the Revolution.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The northern settlements of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-carolina">Province of Carolina</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albemarle-sound">Albemarle Sound</a>, had long been governed separately, and in 1712 the proprietors gave them their own governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-hyde-governor">Edward Hyde</a>. The colony was then fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuscarora-war">Tuscarora War</a>. In 1729 the Crown bought out seven of the eight proprietors, and North Carolina became a royal province.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Without a good deep-water port, the colony grew more slowly than its neighbours, with small farms, tobacco, and naval stores of tar, pitch and turpentine from the longleaf pine forests. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotch-irish-americans">Scots-Irish</a> and German settlers filled the backcountry. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-tryon">William Tryon</a> built an expensive palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-bern">New Bern</a>, the capital from the 1760s, and crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regulator-movement">Regulator movement</a> of backcountry farmers protesting corrupt officials at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alamance">Battle of Alamance</a> in 1771.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah-martin">Josiah Martin</a> fled in 1775. In April 1776 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halifax-resolves">Halifax Resolves</a> made North <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-carolina">Carolina</a> the first colony to instruct its delegates to vote for independence, and it became a state of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-north-carolina.jpg" alt="Province of North Carolina" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: AnonMoos, based on image by Zscout370, AnonMoos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_ensign_of_Great_Britain_(1707%E2%80%931800,_square_canton).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1712 CE – 1776 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>New Bern</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Edward Hyde, William Tryon, Josiah Martin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism, Presbyterianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>North Carolina (colony)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1744&amp;focus=province-of-north-carolina" aria-label="Province of North Carolina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2728 908 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Province of North Carolina lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3048 1164L2935 1168M3048 1164L4997 651M3048 1164L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2935 1168h0M4997 651h0M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3048 1164h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3048 1164h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks New Bern. <a href="/atlas?year=1744&amp;focus=province-of-north-carolina">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
